The output force of a hydraulic cylinder depends on two factors:
The
pressure
supplied by the hydraulic system
The
area
over which that pressure is applied
When a cylinder is extending, fluid pressure acts across the full
piston area
on the cap end of the cylinder. The force produced is the product of pressure and this piston area.
To calculate theoretical force output:
Force (lbs)=Pressure (psi) × Piston Area (in²)
Where the piston area is calculated by:
A = 0.7854 × D²
This formula assumes no losses due to friction or leakage. In practice, the actual force may be slightly lower due to these factors, but this gives a reliable baseline for sizing components and understanding system capabilities.
Example: Calculating the Extension Force of a Cylinder Given its Size and Pressure
To determine the force output of a hydraulic cylinder when it's extending, use the following formula:
P × A = F
Where:
F
= Force in pounds (lbs)
P
= Pressure in pounds per square inch (psi)
A
= Area of the piston in square inches (in²)
Since hydraulic cylinders use round pistons, we calculate piston area using the formula for the area of a circle:
A = 0.7854 ×
D
²
Where
D
is the bore diameter of the cylinder.
Example Calculation
Let’s say the cylinder has a
bore diameter of 2.0 inches
and is subjected to
1000 psi
of hydraulic pressure.
A=0.7854 ×
D
²
A= 0.7854 × (2.0)²
A = 0.7854 × 4 = 3.1416 in.²
F = P × A
F=1000 × 3.1416=3,142 lbs
Result:
The cylinder can theoretically exert
3,142 pounds of force
while extending at 1000 psi.
Additional Practice Problem
Determine the minimum pressure required to lift a known load given:
Load to lift = 5,000 lbs
Cylinder Bore = 3.0 in
Rod Diameter = 1.0 in
We’re lifting, so this is an extension force problem. First, calculate piston area:
A = 0.7854 × (3.0)² = 0.7854 × 9 = 7.069 in²
Now solve for pressure:
P = F / A = 5000 x 7.069 ≈ 707 psi
Answer
A pressure of approximately
707 psi
is required to lift a 5,000 lb load using a 3-inch bore cylinder.
A
big lesson
to learn here is that
larger bore cylinders
reduce the pressure
required
to move a load.